# Chapter 9: Organizing Code with Modules

In systems programming, the way you partition code into files and manage dependencies directly affects build times, cognitive load, and long-term maintainability. Zig’s module system is deliberately minimal—no header files, no preprocessor, no implicit visibility—yet it scales from single-file prototypes to multi-package projects with hundreds of modules. This chapter dissects how Zig’s file-as-module model works, how the compiler resolves imports, and how to structure real-world projects using `build.zig.zon` for package management.

---

## 1. Basic Usage

The “How.” Runnable code snippets showing syntax and minimal API.

### Files Are Modules, Imports Are Paths

Every `.zig` file defines a module. The root module is the file passed to `zig build-exe` (e.g., `main.zig`). Use `@import` to bring another module’s public declarations into scope:

```zig
// math.zig
pub const PI = 3.14159265358979323;
pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) i32 {
    return a + b;
}
pub const Calculator = struct {
    sum: i32,
    fn new() Calculator {
        return .{ .sum = 0 };
    }
};

// main.zig
const math = @import("math.zig");
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() !void {
    const result = math.add(40, 2);
    std.debug.print("40 + 2 = {d}\n", .{result});
    std.debug.print("PI = {d}\n", .{math.PI});
}
```

Compile with `zig build-exe main.zig` – Zig automatically finds `math.zig` in the same directory.

### Visibility: `pub` is the Only Import Gate

Declarations are **private by default**. Only items marked `pub` are visible to importers:

```zig
// library.zig
const secret = 42;          // not accessible from other modules
pub const exposed = 43;     // accessible
fn hidden() void {}         // private
pub fn api() void {}        // public
```

### Subdirectories and Nested Modules

A module can import other modules relative to its own path. Zig does **not** automatically treat directories as namespaces; you must explicitly create an entry point:

```zig
// utils/print.zig
const std = @import("std");
pub fn info(comptime fmt: []const u8, args: anytype) void {
    std.debug.print(fmt, args);
}

// utils/hash.zig
pub fn crc32(data: []const u8) u32 {
    // ...
}

// utils/mod.zig – convention: re-export submodules
pub const print = @import("print.zig");
pub const hash = @import("hash.zig");

// main.zig
const utils = @import("utils/mod.zig");
pub fn main() void {
    utils.print.info("Hello\n", .{});
}
```

Relative imports use `@import("./sub/file.zig")` or `@import("../parent.zig")`. The path must start with `./` or `../`; bare names like `@import("foo")` search the same directory as the current file, then fall back to package roots.

### Package Management with `build.zig.zon`

For external dependencies, Zig 0.13+ uses the **Zig Object Notation (ZON)** file `build.zig.zon` to declare packages. A typical `build.zon.zon` (the canonical name is `build.zig.zon` at project root):

```zon
.{
    .name = "my_server",
    .version = "0.1.0",
    .dependencies = .{
        .httpz = .{
            .url = "https://github.com/karlseguin/httpz/archive/refs/tags/0.5.0.tar.gz",
            .hash = "1220a8b3c2d4e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4a5b6c7d8e9f0a",
        },
    },
    .paths = .{
        "build.zig",
        "build.zig.zon",
        "src",
        "LICENSE",
    },
}
```

In `build.zig`, you add the dependency to your executable:

```zig
// build.zig (partial)
const exe = b.addExecutable(.{
    .name = "my_server",
    .root_source_file = .{ .path = "src/main.zig" },
    .target = target,
    .optimize = optimize,
});
const httpz = b.dependency("httpz", .{});
exe.root_module.addImport("httpz", httpz.module("httpz"));
```

Then in your code:

```zig
const httpz = @import("httpz");
```

The hash in `build.zig.zon` ensures reproducible builds – Zig verifies the downloaded tarball matches the hash.

